<p>
  Purchasing covers everything you buy: order from a supplier, receive what arrives, enter the
  supplier's invoice (a <strong>bill</strong>) and pay it. Suppliers are ordinary companies or
  contacts; they're tagged <em>supplier</em> the first time you buy from them.
</p>

<h2>Requests for quotation</h2>
<p>
  Not sure of the price yet? Send a <strong>request for quotation</strong>
  (<code>RFQ-…</code>) from <.link navigate="/rfqs">Requests for quotation</.link>
  or from the supplier's page (<strong>New → Request
  for quotation</strong>). Print it or save it as PDF to send. When the supplier answers, edit the lines
  with their prices and click <strong>Supplier replied</strong>; then
  <strong>Create purchase order</strong>
  turns it into an order at those prices.
</p>

<h2>Purchase orders</h2>
<ol>
  <li>
    Go to <.link navigate="/purchase-orders/new">Purchase orders → + Purchase order</.link>, pick the
    supplier and add lines. Picking a product fills in its <strong>purchase cost</strong>.
  </li>
  <li><strong>Print / PDF</strong> to send it, then <strong>Mark ordered</strong>.</li>
  <li>
    When goods arrive, click <strong>Receive</strong>. Enter what actually came and the supplier's
    delivery note number: deliveries can arrive in several parts, and the order shows
    <em>Partially received</em>
    until everything is in. Stock-tracked products go straight into the
    warehouse, and each receipt gets a printable <strong>goods received note</strong>
    (<code>GRN-…</code>) under <em>Receipts</em>
    for whoever checked the goods to sign.
  </li>
</ol>

<.callout>
  The <strong>Print</strong>
  menu on a purchase order (and on its bill) prints the order or any of its
  goods received notes, one at a time or all together.
</.callout>

<h2>Bills</h2>
<p>
  A bill is the supplier's invoice to you. Create it from a purchase order with <strong>Enter bill</strong>, or enter one directly under
  <.link navigate="/bills/new">Bills</.link>
  (rent, utilities, software…).
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Type the <strong>supplier's invoice number</strong> so you can find it later.</li>
  <li>
    For lines that aren't products, pick the <strong>account</strong>
    they belong to (rent, marketing…).
    Leave it empty to file them under <em>Other expenses</em>.
  </li>
  <li>
    An admin <strong>approves</strong>
    the bill when it's correct. Only then is it owed and payable.
    Drafts can be edited or deleted freely.
  </li>
  <li>
    Stock bought on a bill without a purchase order arrives in the default warehouse when the bill is
    approved.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Paying suppliers</h2>
<p>
  On an approved bill, click <strong>Record payment</strong>. Partial payments are fine; the bill shows
  <em>Paid</em>
  once nothing is left. Bills past their due date show as <em>Overdue</em>.
  <.link navigate="/bills">Bills</.link>
  shows what you owe, what's due this week and what's late.
</p>

<.callout>
  The price on the bill doesn't match the order? Just correct the bill before approving it.
  Zippiker books the difference to cost of sales, so your stock keeps the value it arrived with.
</.callout>
<.callout kind={:warning}>
  An approved bill can't be edited. If it's wrong and unpaid, an admin can void it; the books and any
  stock it brought in are corrected automatically.
</.callout>
